Stage 3: Spring Boot

Now imagine Spring Boot.

Instead of writing

PaymentFactory.getPaymentService("UPI");

Spring creates the object automatically.

@Service
public class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService

Then

@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final PaymentService paymentService;

    public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
        this.paymentService = paymentService;
    }
}

Spring does something conceptually similar to this behind the scenes:

PaymentService payment =
        new UpiPaymentService();

OrderService order =
        new OrderService(payment);

except it manages object creation, lifecycle, configuration, and injection for you.

The key realization

Notice what never changed across all three versions:

public class OrderService {

    private PaymentService paymentService;
}
